    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Have these forgetfulness problems been a change from how you used to be, or have you always been this forgetful?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Change? No, no, I wouldn't call it a change. It's pretty much the story of my life, honestly. Even way back as a boy in grade school, I was always that kid, you know? The one who just couldn’t stay in his seat to save his life. I was always blurting out answers before the teacher even finished the question, and getting notes sent home constantly for talking too much. \n\nSpeaking of those notes, my third-grade teacher, Mrs. Higgins... she used to actually pin them to my jacket. With safety pins! Just right there on the lapel so I couldn't pretend I lost it on the walk home. Can you imagine a teacher doing that nowadays? The parents would throw an absolute fit, probably call the news stations! But back then, my mother would just unpin it, shake her head, and make me go peel potatoes for supper. We ate a lot of potatoes back then. Mashed, boiled, fried... my uncle grew them out on his farm and he'd send us these massive burlap sacks every fall. Have you ever tried to drag a fifty-pound sack of potatoes across a linoleum floor when you're eight years old? It builds character, I'll tell you that much. The dust gets everywhere, though. \n\nBut, uh... anyway, what was the question? Oh, right, if this is new. No, Doc, I've pretty much always been this way.",
